---
wmxdsl: 1
title: The Last Signalman
lang: en
---

\font[family="Lyon Text", src=/fonts/lyon-text.woff2, weight=400]
\font[family="Lyon Text", src=/fonts/lyon-text-italic.woff2, weight=400, italic]
\font[family="Lyon Display", src=/fonts/lyon-display.woff2, weight=600]

\palette[name=ink, paper=#14171a, ink=#ece8df, muted=#8e949a, accent=#d97b41, rule=#2a2f35]

\grid[name=read, cols=12, gutter=24px, margin-x=64px, max=1440px, baseline=28px, rows=6, body=4-9]
\grid[name=read, at=tablet, cols=8, gutter=20px, margin-x=40px, baseline=27px, body=2-7]
\grid[name=read, at=phone, cols=4, gutter=16px, margin-x=20px, baseline=26px, body=all]

%% GAP 10: classic book setting. Indent every paragraph, no space between, but NOT the
%% first paragraph of a chapter or the one after a subhead. The spec has indent= and
%% nothing that suppresses it, so every chapter opening is indented.
\style[name=body, family="Lyon Text, serif", indent=1.2em, space-after=0]
\style[name=headline, family="Lyon Display, serif"]
\style[name=leadin, case=small-caps, tracking=0.04em]
\style[name=chapter-no, extends=kicker, size=16px]

%% GAP 2: I want a huge numeral and a small label inside the stat box. Bare paragraphs
%% always use the story style and there is no generic styled paragraph, so the only
%% route is wrapping the whole paragraph in \span, which is a character style
%% and cannot carry size-dependent leading, spacing or alignment.
\style[name=bignum, family="Lyon Display, serif", size=72px, color=accent]

\parent[name=chapter, grid=read, palette=ink, height=screen, snap=soft, enter=fade, stagger=150ms]

\folio[position=top-left, progress]{The Last Signalman}

\story[name=main]{
  \dropcap[lines=4]
  \span[style=leadin]{The lever frame} at Abbotsbury Junction has forty-two levers, and
  Tom Whitlow can tell you which ones stick in the cold.

  He has worked the box since 1987. When it closes next spring the line will be run from
  a control centre ninety miles away.

  \figure[src=/sig/levers.jpg, alt="Painted signal levers in a row", cols=10-12,
          cols@tablet=all, cols@phone=all, enter=fade]{
    \caption{Red for stop signals, black for points, blue for locks.}
  }

  The work is mostly waiting, and then it is not.

  \framebreak

  \subhead[span=all]{A language of bells}

  Signal boxes talk to each other in bell codes. Two beats, pause, one: is the line clear
  for an express passenger train?

  %% GAP 9: this pull quote straddles both columns of a balanced two-column frame.
  %% Its anchor may land in column 2 after balancing, but it cuts into column 1, which
  %% is already laid out. The spec does not say what happens.
  \pullquote[cols=5-8, wrap=rect, wrap-side=both, hide@phone]{
    You learn to hear a mistake before you see it.
  }

  He learned them from a man who learned them in 1951. Nobody will learn them from him.

  \sidebar[cols=10-12, inset=1bl, border=rule, cols@tablet=all, cols@phone=all]{
    \title{Mechanical boxes left}
    \span[style=bignum]{83}

    Down from more than 10,000 in 1948.
  }

  \framebreak

  \figure[src=/sig/night.jpg, alt="The signal box lit at night", cols=all, bleed=both, ratio=21:9,
          ratio@phone=4:3]{
    \caption{The box is staffed around the clock, in three shifts.}
  }

  The night shift is the one he will miss.

  \embed[provider=youtube, id=a1B2c3D4e5F, title="Bell codes demonstrated", side=full]{
    \caption{Whitlow demonstrates the codes for an express and a stopping train.}
  }

  He will stay until the last day. Someone has to pull the last lever. \endmark
}

\scene[name=opener, grid=read, height=screen, snap=soft]{
  %% GAP 4: bleed=right reaches the viewport's right edge, but rows=all stops at
  %% margin-y, so the image floats with a band of paper above and below.
  %% There is no vertical bleed.
  \figure[src=/sig/box.jpg, alt="A timber signal box beside a single track", cols=7-12, rows=all,
          bleed=right, focus="40% 50%", cols@phone=all, rows@phone=1-3, loading=eager]

  \frame[cols=1-6, rows=2-5, cols@phone=all, rows@phone=4-6, valign=center, enter=rise]{
    \kicker{Long read}
    \headline{The Last Signalman}
    \deck{For 140 years a person in a wooden box has kept trains apart on this line.}
    \byline{Rohan Mehta}
    \meta{24 min read · [Chapter two](#two) · [Chapter three](#three)}
  }
}

\scene[name=one, grid=read]{
  \frame[story=main]
}

\scene[name=two, parent=chapter]{
  %% GAP 7: I want this whole block centered. align lives on styles only, so I would need
  %% a centered clone of every style I use here: chapter-no, headline, deck.
  \frame[cols=3-10, rows=3-4, cols@phone=all]{
    \kicker[style=chapter-no]{Two}
    \headline{A language of bells}
  }
}

\scene[name=two-text, grid=read]{
  \frame[story=main, cols=2-9, columns=2, cols@tablet=all, cols@phone=all, columns@phone=1]
}

\scene[name=three, parent=chapter]{
  \frame[cols=3-10, rows=3-4, cols@phone=all]{
    \kicker[style=chapter-no]{Three}
    \headline{Nights}
  }
}

\scene[name=three-text, grid=read]{
  \frame[story=main]
  \frame{
    \rule
    \bio{Rohan Mehta writes about infrastructure and the people who run it.}
  }
}
